ve only considered top  and bottom faces but they're gonna be  three pairs of opposite faces on a cube  we've just Illustrated top and bottom  but we can also do the same sort of  thing for the front and back faces and  we could do the same sort of thing for  the left and right faces where the aunts  in these faces rotate positions with  each other so how many different ways  have we just figured out well they're  four different ways for each pair of  opposite faces and there are three kinds  of opposite faces that makes for 4 times  3 which equals 12 ways the aunts do not  collide but there is more there's  another way that the aunts could not  collide which is that all eight of the  aunts can move in a loop a closed path  that goes through every vertex exactly  once is called a Hamiltonian cycle now  to preempt a comment it will go through  every vertex exactly once except for the  vertex that's both at the start at the  end which is visited twice so with that  caveat we have the definition of a  cameltoe nyan cycle so we want to figure  out how many Hamiltonian cycles there  are for the cube so this will be a  little bit more difficult to show you so  rather than going through this 3d model  I'm actually going to flatten out the  cube this is known as a cubicle graph  and it's a 2d version of the cube so  let's illustrate the number of  Hamiltonian cycles let me clean up this  diagram and now I'm gonna tell you there  are actually six undirected Hamiltonian  cycles you can imagine if you start at  one vertex there'll be three choices and  then for the second vertex that you go  to there will be two more choices so  here's one example of a Hamiltonian  cycle now let me go ahead and illustrate  all of the other ones as well  now this front face involves three  different edges  that of going through this loop but  Udall said the front face have exactly  two of its edges involved in a loop so  here are the remaining Hamiltonian  cycles so these are six undirected  Hamiltonian cycles for each of these we  can actually place directions to create  the ways that the ants might possibly  flow on the cube  these are directed Hamiltonian cycles  now this is one way that we could draw  the arrows but we can also draw the  arrows in the opposite direction  therefore for each undirected  Hamiltonian cycle we have two possible  directions for the loop so this means  since each loop can be traversed in two  directions there are twelve directed  Hamiltonian cycles in other words twelve  ways that the ants could move so that  they would not collide so now let's  calculate the answer there are 24 total  ways that the ants will not collide we  have 12 cases from case one and twelve  cases from case two this is out of a  total of 65 61 possible ways the ants  could move therefore the probability  that there's no collision will be 24  divided by 65 61 which equals 8 out of  20 187 which is approximately 0.37%  it's a very slim chance that the ants  will not collide but it involves very  interesting geometrical loops that are  on the cube did you figure it out thanks  for watching this video please subscribe  to my channel I make videos on Mac you  can catch free on my blog mind your  decisions that you can follow on  Facebook Google+ and patreon you can  catch her on social media at pressure  Larker and if you liked this video  please check out my books there are  links in the video description
 

